## User preferences

- when the user said "merge each of my branches with the upstream newton repo each night" and "my research pressure field contact work is preserved and the tests still run at the end of this automation" -> preservation of research branches and post-merge validation are hard requirements, not nice-to-haves [Task 1]
- when the user said "i want this to run every morning at 6am" -> default the merge automation to a `6:00 AM` schedule unless the user changes it [Task 1]
- when the user said "use the internet to find the right format" and pointed to a Codex automation article -> ground automation structure in current Codex docs/article guidance instead of inventing a shape from memory [Task 1]
- when the user asked "how can we get the automation to keep going and fix the failures until the tests pass" and then said "yes implement that" -> prefer a bounded queue-and-repair design over a single unbounded automation run [Task 2]
- when the user said "yes lets test it now" and later "yep go ahead" -> live-test automation changes against the real queue instead of leaving them as untested scaffolding [Task 3]
- when the user asked to "run the rebase now and then figure out why the nightly rebase is not running every night and resolving itself" -> treat that as both an immediate execution request and a scheduler/root-cause investigation, not a status-only question [Task 4]
- when the user says "rebase" in this repo, verify the actual implementation before echoing the term back; the repo-owned workflow is an upstream merge runner plus bounded repair loop [Task 4]

## Reusable knowledge

- The merge automation lives at `.codex/automations/nightly-upstream-merge/automation.toml` and schedules `scripts/nightly_upstream_merge.py` with cron `0 6 * * *` [Task 1]
- The runner uses disposable worktrees so it does not mutate the current checkout while merging `upstream/main` into active origin branches [Task 1]
- The durable failure queue is `.codex/automations/nightly-upstream-merge/failures.json`; items record branch, failure type, logs, report path, attempt count, and validation commands [Task 2]
- The repair workflow is owned by `scripts/nightly_upstream_repair.py` with `prepare`, `finalize`, and `status`, and active state is stored in `.codex/automations/nightly-upstream-repair/current_repair.json` [Task 2]
- Repair runs are intentionally bounded to three attempts and scheduled hourly from `6:15 AM` through `11:15 AM` via `.codex/automations/nightly-upstream-repair/automation.toml` [Task 2]
- The pressure-field validation slice that mattered in live repairs was `uv run --extra dev -m newton.tests -k pressure` plus `uv run --extra examples -m newton.examples.contacts.example_hydro_pressure_slice --test --viewer null --num-frames 1 --shape box` [Task 3]
- The real `research/pressure-field` regression was a missing `@wp.func` annotation on `hydroelastic_mode_from_flags()` in `newton/_src/geometry/sdf_hydroelastic.py`; aligning it with the decorated helper in `narrow_phase.py` fixed the Warp codegen failure [Task 3]
- `protomotions` was repaired by merging it onto the already repaired pressure-field line and keeping the newer hydro implementation, not by repeatedly forcing a direct upstream merge into the stale branch [Task 3]
- The stale Windows task was still calling `scripts/nightly_sync_report.ps1`; the durable scheduler fix was to register two `wsl.exe` tasks that run `python3 scripts/run_codex_automation.py <automation.toml>` from `/home/jkuzm/biomotions/newton` [Task 4]
- `scripts/run_codex_automation.py` is now the non-interactive bridge from Task Scheduler to `codex exec`, and `scripts/register_nightly_sync_task.ps1` is the repo-owned registration entrypoint for both nightly tasks [Task 4]
- For this fork, `.codex/automations/nightly-upstream-merge/automation.toml` should invoke `uv run --script scripts/nightly_upstream_merge.py --push --skip-main-sync`; fork-only `origin/main` should not make the nightly job fail [Task 4]
- The shared 2026-04-24 conflict in `newton/_src/geometry/__init__.py` was resolved by keeping upstream `contact_match` exports while preserving `HydroelasticContactWorkflow` and `HydroelasticType`; the final rerun ended with `Queued items: 0` [Task 4]

## Failures and how to do differently

- A generic "merge succeeded" report was not enough for this repo; branch-specific validators are required, especially for `research/pressure-field` and branches derived from it [Task 1][Task 3]
- Keep failure reports short and log-oriented. The first report format was too verbose; point to per-branch logs and the queue entry instead of dumping everything into the summary [Task 1]
- If `pre-commit` reformats unrelated scratch files in a repair worktree, exclude or revert that noise before finalizing the repair commit [Task 2]
- Queue state can look inconsistent if `prepare` and `status` are inspected in parallel; read status after `prepare` finishes and treat `current_repair.json` as the single active-item lock [Task 2]
- When a queued branch lags behind another repaired research branch, direct upstream merge may be the wrong repair shape; merge the stale branch onto the repaired line, rerun the exact queued validators, then finalize/push [Task 3]
- If the nightly run suddenly stops self-healing, inspect the scheduled task action before debugging merge logic; this repo had a scheduler drift where Windows still invoked the legacy PowerShell report script instead of the Codex automation path [Task 4]
- PowerShell/Git path handling against `\\\\wsl.localhost\\...` can trigger dubious-ownership and wrong-path failures; normalize to the real WSL repo path and verify Task Scheduler shows `Execute: wsl.exe` after re-registration [Task 4]
- A first live run can fail only because `origin/main` is fork-only and not fast-forwardable; do not treat that as evidence the research-branch automation is broken if `--skip-main-sync` is the intended policy for this fork [Task 4]

