   227	
   228	- The merge automation lives at `.codex/automations/nightly-upstream-merge/automation.toml` and schedules `scripts/nightly_upstream_merge.py` with cron `0 6 * * *` [Task 1]
   229	- The runner uses disposable worktrees so it does not mutate the current checkout while merging `upstream/main` into active origin branches [Task 1]
   230	- The durable failure queue is `.codex/automations/nightly-upstream-merge/failures.json`; items record branch, failure type, logs, report path, attempt count, and validation commands [Task 2]
   231	- The repair workflow is owned by `scripts/nightly_upstream_repair.py` with `prepare`, `finalize`, and `status`, and active state is stored in `.codex/automations/nightly-upstream-repair/current_repair.json` [Task 2]
   232	- Repair runs are intentionally bounded to three attempts and scheduled hourly from `6:15 AM` through `11:15 AM` via `.codex/automations/nightly-upstream-repair/automation.toml` [Task 2]
   233	- The pressure-field validation slice that mattered in live repairs was `uv run --extra dev -m newton.tests -k pressure` plus `uv run --extra examples -m newton.examples.contacts.example_hydro_pressure_slice --test --viewer null --num-frames 1 --shape box` [Task 3]
   234	- The real `research/pressure-field` regression was a missing `@wp.func` annotation on `hydroelastic_mode_from_flags()` in `newton/_src/geometry/sdf_hydroelastic.py`; aligning it with the decorated helper in `narrow_phase.py` fixed the Warp codegen failure [Task 3]
   235	- `protomotions` was repaired by merging it onto the already repaired pressure-field line and keeping the newer hydro implementation, not by repeatedly forcing a direct upstream merge into the stale branch [Task 3]
   236	- The stale Windows task was still calling `scripts/nightly_sync_report.ps1`; the durable scheduler fix was to register two `wsl.exe` tasks that run `python3 scripts/run_codex_automation.py <automation.toml>` from `/home/jkuzm/biomotions/newton` [Task 4]
   237	- `scripts/run_codex_automation.py` is now the non-interactive bridge from Task Scheduler to `codex exec`, and `scripts/register_nightly_sync_task.ps1` is the repo-owned registration entrypoint for both nightly tasks [Task 4]
   238	- For this fork, `.codex/automations/nightly-upstream-merge/automation.toml` should invoke `uv run --script scripts/nightly_upstream_merge.py --push --skip-main-sync`; fork-only `origin/main` should not make the nightly job fail [Task 4]
   239	- The shared 2026-04-24 conflict in `newton/_src/geometry/__init__.py` was resolved by keeping upstream `contact_match` exports while preserving `HydroelasticContactWorkflow` and `HydroelasticType`; the final rerun ended with `Queued items: 0` [Task 4]
   240	
   241	## Failures and how to do differently
   242	
   243	- A generic "merge succeeded" report was not enough for this repo; branch-specific validators are required, especially for `research/pressure-field` and branches derived from it [Task 1][Task 3]
   244	- Keep failure reports short and log-oriented. The first report format was too verbose; point to per-branch logs and the queue entry instead of dumping everything into the summary [Task 1]
   245	- If `pre-commit` reformats unrelated scratch files in a repair worktree, exclude or revert that noise before finalizing the repair commit [Task 2]
   246	- Queue state can look inconsistent if `prepare` and `status` are inspected in parallel; read status after `prepare` finishes and treat `current_repair.json` as the single active-item lock [Task 2]
   247	- When a queued branch lags behind another repaired research branch, direct upstream merge may be the wrong repair shape; merge the stale branch onto the repaired line, rerun the exact queued validators, then finalize/push [Task 3]
   248	- If the nightly run suddenly stops self-healing, inspect the scheduled task action before debugging merge logic; this repo had a scheduler drift where Windows still invoked the legacy PowerShell report script instead of the Codex automation path [Task 4]
   249	- PowerShell/Git path handling against `\\\\wsl.localhost\\...` can trigger dubious-ownership and wrong-path failures; normalize to the real WSL repo path and verify Task Scheduler shows `Execute: wsl.exe` after re-registration [Task 4]

